Found in a bug hunt; reproduced by the finder and independently
re-reproduced by me.

Git.pm:470-475 (_config_string) returns what libgit2 hands back -- the raw
bytes of the config file -- with no crossing through App::karr::Encoding.
Every other octet crossing in that class goes through it
(read_ref_with_oid calls from_octets); this one does not. A non-ASCII
user.name enters the program as a byte string treated as characters.

    git config user.name 'Ünicode Tester'
    karr create "..."
    karr log
    2026-08-12T16:11:11Z  Ãnicode Tester create task#1 backlog

The value is then JSON-encoded and written to the log ref, where to_octets
encodes it a SECOND time: c3 83 c2 9c, i.e. Ü encoded twice. The task ref
written by the same command is correct (c3 9c), so the corruption is
specific to the identity.

Two layers of cost:

- output is wrong wherever an identity is printed, including --json, which
  an agent parses.
- the mojibake is written into refs on a board that karr init correctly
  stamped as encoding version 2. So board_is_legacy_encoded is false,
  repair_mojibake never runs on read, and `karr repair` answers "Board
  encoding is already at version 2; nothing to repair." There is no path
  from inside karr back to the correct text, and every new command adds
  more of it.

git_user_email has the same gap (it feeds the lock holder identity and
Cmd::Pick's $email), and _run_git's captured stderr (Git.pm:1930) is the
same class -- raw octets printed straight to a :encoding(UTF-8) handle.
